## 1. Introduction
In the past few decades, diverse advanced porous materials composed of inorganic and organic materials, such as zeolites [1], porous organic polymers [2], metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [3], covalent organic frameworks (COFs) [4], and supramolecular frameworks [5], have been widely favored in many scientific and technological fields. Among them, MOFs and COFs, as a class of emerging and booming ordered porous materials, have attracted extensive research interest and consideration since they were first discovered by Yaghi's group in 1995 and 2005, respectively [6,7]. MOFs, which are composed of organic linkers and transition metal ions as secondary building units (SBUs) through self-assembly, are crystalline porous inorganic–organic hybrid materials with periodic network structures [8–10]. Moreover, COFs are a class of porous materials consist of light elements (H, B, C, N and O) connected with organic monomers via strong covalent bonds [11,12]. In recent years, credited with prominent flexible designability, outstanding surface areas, remarkably high thermal stability, and topological structure diversity, MOFs and COFs have shown widely potential applications in gas adsorption/separation [13–15], catalysis [16,17], sensing [18,19], chromatographic separation [20,21] and energy storage [22,23].
