Recently, to enhance the properties of original single materials, increasing attention has been given to the combination of functional components to obtain core-shell or doped composites, which can combine the physical and chemical properties and functions of individual components. At present, the focus of large amounts of studies on MOFs has shifted from early synthesis to research on their performance and applications [24,25]. However, MOFs still have a relatively limited application scope because of some inherent defects. For example, the relative strength of the coordination bond often remains a grand challenge in the practical applications of most MOFs, particularly under extremely severe conditions [26–28]. To surmount these problems, researchers have begun to combine MOFs with other functional materials to prepare composites, such as MOFs/silica [29], MOFs/polymers [30], MOFs/enzymes [31], MOFs/carbon materials [32], and MOFs/MOFs [33], which not only possess the structural characteristics of the original
components but also exhibit some extra properties to meet the actual needs. For instance, our group recently reported MOF-magnetic carbon nanosheets and core-shell MOF/polymer composites for largely enhanced sample pretreatment and chromatographic separation [34,35]. Similar to MOFs, the development of COFs has also gradually realized the transition from synthesis to applications [36]. Compared with MOFs, COFs also have some intrinsic limitations, such as the insolubility and poor dispersion of microcrystalline COFs powders in most solvents [37,38]. Tremendous endeavors have been made to decorate COFs with other functional materials to develop COFs-based hybrid materials, including metal nanoparticles [39], carbon materials [40], polymers [41], different COFs [42], and quantum dots [43], thereby avoiding defects to improve properties of the original materials.
In addition, for MOFs and COFs, the combination of functional components can be realized by cross functionalization with each other. On the one hand, if the SBUs of MOFs are integrated into the building units of COFs, the composites will show the properties of both MOFs and COFs, which will inspire and promote the further development of MOFs and COFs, and bring some novel functions that cannot be realized by a single component [44,45]. Taking the development of metal-covalent organic frameworks (MCOFs) as an example, the restriction that COFs suffer from the lack of active metal centers can be addressed by introducing single metal ions or mononuclear metal complexes into highly stable COFs [45]. On the other hand, to maximize their inherent characteristics and endow additional functions, it is a good strategy to combine MOFs and COFs materials for a synergistic effect [38]. For example, Fu et al. [46] synthesized a new class of COF@MOF composite membranes, which exceed the Robeson upper bound of polymer membranes for mixture separation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas pairs and are one of the best gas separation MOF membranes reported thus far. Since then, the combination of MOFs and COFs has become a new research direction and has been increasingly studied to obtain MOF/COF composites with excellent properties for different fields (Fig. 1).
In recent years, some reviews have been related to discussing the synthetic strategies and applications of MOF/COF-based composites, but only some chapters have focused on MOF/COF composites [38,47–52]. In fact, in the past few years (2016–2021), significant achievements have been made in MOF/COF composites, it is therefore time for a comprehensive review of synthetic strategies, recent progresses and future perspectives of MOF/COF composites. At the time of which this review is written, Wu et al. [53] and Li et al. [54] presented the advancement of MOF/COF composites from their synthesis to applications. In contrast, we further systematically summarize and expand the integration methods between MOFs and COFs according to the adopted synthetic strategies, with emphasis on latest progress in analytical chemistry. Firstly, we comprehensively present synthetic strategies that combine MOFs and COFs, such as dynamic covalent chemistry strategy, direct condensation, post-synthetic modification, \(\pi-\pi\) stacking interaction, MOF-in-COF assembly strategy, and modular synthesis strategy. Secondly, the recent progress of MOF/COF composites in analytical applications and other fields is summarized in detail. Finally, we discuss the current technical challenges that need to be solved, together with future perspectives on MOF/ COF composites. Despite the initial progress in some areas, MOF/ COF composites are still in their infancy and need to be further studied and refined. Therefore, this review aims to provide some insights for future in-depth studies on the combination of MOFs and COFs and their applications, as well as be able to draw more attention and research on MOF/COF composites.
## 2. The synthetic strategies of MOF/COF composites
To realize the combination of MOFs and COFs, a series of studies have recently been carried out to indicate its feasibility. The resulting composites not only inherit plenty of their own characteristics, such as abundant porous structure, tunable pore size and large specific surface area, but also provide some additional features through the synergistic effects of individual components. In this section, the integration methods of two classes of MOF/COF composites will be discussed (Fig. 2): (1) Dynamic covalent chemistry is introduced into MOFs materials to prepare a range of composites with COFs units, and (2) fabrication of hierarchical hybrid MOF/COF composites by using different synthetic strategies
## 2.1. Dynamic covalent chemistry strategy
Dynamic covalent chemistry, which has played a tremendous boost in the synthesis and applications of COFs, is the practice of applying thermodynamic equilibriums to molecular assemblies [55]. In recent years, researchers have found that the combination of metal cluster chemistry with imine bonds and B–O bonds used to construct COFs can generate a series of composites with COFs units in MOFs, bringing new opportunities for the design and synthesis of novel MOFs.
## 2.1.1. Imine formation
In 2016, inspired by a series of Ti(IV)-oxo clusters compounds, Naghi's group proposed a strategy to prepare novel Ti-MOFs (MOF-901) by functionalizing the SBUs of MOFs and then combining them with COFs constituents (Fig. 3a) [56]. Specifically, this strategy first prepared isolated clusters of titanium(IV) isopropoxide, which were then functionalized with 4-aminobenzoic acid. Finally, after confirming the successful synthesis of amine-functionalized metal clusters by characterization, they were linked with the 1,4-benzenedialdehyde (BDA) components by imine condensation to construct MOF-901. The MOF-901 is the first instance that combines the chemistry of metal clusters with dynamic organic covalent bonding, resulting in a new crystalline and extended titanium framework, which is rarely used in MOFs. Soon after, the research group further engineered a kind of Ti-MOF with water stability and chemically stability, named MOF-902 (Fig. 3a) [57]. This MOF-902 has the same isoreticular structure as the previously synthesized MOF-901, the only difference is that the highly conjugated 4,4'-biphenyldicarboxaldehyde (BPDA) was selected for the functionalization process.
Similarly, Zhou's group reported a method of sequential linker stabilization and reinstallation to expand the unit cell size of MOFs while manipulating the framework structure and infiltrating each other (Fig. 3b) [58]. First, a stable and non-interpenetrated Zr-based MOFs was synthesized as a template. Second, unstable linkers with imine bonds were introduced into the structure by post-synthesis to destroy the stability of Zr-MOF. Finally, the progressive dissociation of imine-based linkers and reinstallation, enable the holes to be expanded and contracted by reinstalling longer or shorter linkers, resulting in a range of non-interpenetrated iso-reticular Zr-MOFs. Significantly, the idea of using dynamic covalent chemistry to control the properties of synthesized MOFs in this work will open up new opportunities for MOFs and other fields.
## 2.1.2. Boron-oxygen formation
In 2019, Wang et al. [59] developed a novel pore-space allocation technique via the coassembly strategy of COF-MOF, which facilitated the preparation of a series of new pore distribution materials (Fig. 3c). The construction progress of the method involving pyridine-4-boronic acid was initially introduced into the
