## 2.2. Fabrication of hierarchical hybrid MOF/COF composites
2.2. Fabrication of hierarchical hybrid MOF/COF compositesTo date, a great quantity of hierarchical hybrid MOF/COF composites have been successfully prepared by different synthetic strategies, which can be classified into two types: the bulk mixed-component type and the epitaxial core-shell type according to the assembled structural patterns [60]. Herein, we systematically introduce different strategies for combining MOFs and COFs to fabricate hybrid materials, including direct condensation, postsynthetic modification, \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking and others.
## 2.2.1. Direct condensation strategy
2.2.1. Direct condensation strategyCondensation is a chemical reaction in which two or more organic molecules interact to form one single molecule with a loss of entropy [61]. So far, most hybrid MOF/COF composites have been synthesized by introducing amino functionalized MOFs during the synthesis of COFs, that is, through the formation of Schiff bases via the direct condensation of amine-aldehyde between the two components, leading to the formation of a series MOF@COF hybrid structures as a novel kind of hybrid materials [62–66]. For example, Zhang et al. [62] prepared the first covalently linked MOF/COF hybrid materials, for which TpPa-1-COF was selected to synthesize hybrid materials in view of the typical COF structure of the Schiff base (Fig. 4a). Firstly, MOFs \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{UiO}-66)\) was prepared by stirring and heating the DMF solution containing \(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\) and 2- aminoterephthalic acid ligands at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Subsequently, various amounts of MOF were added to the reaction system for synthesizing COFs with 1,3,5-triformorphloroglucinol (Tp) and phenylenediamine (Pa) as monomers to prepare MOF/COF hybrid materials. It is worth mentioning that a slight excess of Tp plays a very important role in the synthesis of hybrid materials, as it allows a better condensation reaction with the MOFs components, linking the two together. All the peaks of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66/TpPa-1-COF were consistent with the PXRD spectra, and combined with their FT-IR spectra, it can be considered that MOF/COF hybrid materials have been successfully synthesized.Then Zhang and colleagues also synthesized the first class of hybrid MOF@COF core-shell materials in the same year, that is, a rod-shaped MOFs \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{MIL}-68)\) have been synthesized using a solvothermal method as the core substrate, and then the monomer (tris(4-formylphenyl)amine, TFPA) for synthesizing TPA-COF was selected to be surface functionalized by Schiff-base reaction to form aldehyde-functionalized MOFs [63]. Finally, TFPA was covalently condensed with another monomer (tris(4-aminophenyl)amine, TAPA) for the synthesis of TPA-COF to form a hybrid MOF@COF core-shell materials (Fig. 4b). Similarly, Li and coworkers prepared a MOF@COF core-shell composite with better stability by one-step synthesis method, in which the MOFs \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{MIL}-101)\) with poor water stability was selected as the core and the hydrophobic COFs (NTU-COF) with better stability was selected as the protective shell, successfully changing their porosity and hydrophilic hydrophobic performances (Fig. 4c) [64]. The preparation process included first adding a certain amount of synthesized MOF to the suspension for the preparation of COFs and mixing well, and then adding different amounts of 4-formylbenzeneboronic acid (FPBA) and 1,3,5-tris(4- aminophenyl)-benzene (TAPB), thereby allowing a condensation reaction between the aminated MOFs and a slight excess of FPBA to covalently immobilize FPBA on the surface of MOFs. And the unreacted FPBA and TAPB further reacted with each other to form a stable COFs shell, thus a series of MOF@COF core-shell composites were successfully fabricated. In summary, most hybrid MOF/COF composites prepared by direct condensation mainly rely on Schiff base reaction, i.e., the formation of imine bonds, primarily because of their good dynamic covalent properties, which makes it easier for COFs to crystallize on the surface of MOFs.
Then Zhang and colleagues also synthesized the first class of hybrid MOF@COF core-shell materials in the same year, that is, a rod-shaped MOFs \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{MIL}-68)\) have been synthesized using a solvothermal method as the core substrate, and then the monomer (tris(4-formylphenyl)amine, TFPA) for synthesizing TPA-COF was selected to be surface functionalized by Schiff-base reaction to form aldehyde-functionalized MOFs [63]. Finally, TFPA was covalently condensed with another monomer (tris(4-aminophenyl)amine, TAPA) for the synthesis of TPA-COF to form a hybrid MOF@COF core-shell materials (Fig. 4b). Similarly, Li and coworkers prepared a MOF@COF core-shell composite with better stability by one-step synthesis method, in which the MOFs \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{MIL}-101)\) with poor water stability was selected as the core and the hydrophobic COFs (NTU-COF) with better stability was selected as the protective shell, successfully changing their porosity and hydrophilic hydrophobic performances (Fig. 4c) [64]. The preparation process included first adding a certain amount of synthesized MOF to the suspension for the preparation of COFs and mixing well, and then adding different amounts of 4-formylbenzeneboronic acid (FPBA) and 1,3,5-tris(4- aminophenyl)-benzene (TAPB), thereby allowing a condensation reaction between the aminated MOFs and a slight excess of FPBA to covalently immobilize FPBA on the surface of MOFs. And the unreacted FPBA and TAPB further reacted with each other to form a stable COFs shell, thus a series of MOF@COF core-shell composites were successfully fabricated. In summary, most hybrid MOF/COF composites prepared by direct condensation mainly rely on Schiff base reaction, i.e., the formation of imine bonds, primarily because of their good dynamic covalent properties, which makes it easier for COFs to crystallize on the surface of MOFs.
## 2.2.2. Post-synthetic modification strategy
2.2.2. Post-synthetic modification strategyModification introduces specific functional groups into MOFs or COFs without significantly changing the lattice structure, to improve their properties or enhance specific applications [67]. Therefore, the most common method of post-synthetic modification, covalent modification, is of enormous importance for the further advance of a new way to construct covalently bonded hybrid MOF/COF composites. For example, Li et al. [68] presented a novel approach to synthesize a class of hierarchical porous hybrid MOF/COF composites by post-synthetic modification of COFs. First, covalent triazine-based frameworks (CTF-1) were synthesized by the thermal oxidation method, and then functionalized by chemical grafting modification with 4- aminobenzoic acid (R-CTF-1). Finally, a certain amount of MOFs \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{MIL}-125(\mathrm{Ti})\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66(Zr)) and B-CTF-1 powder were added together, and hierarchical MOF/ COF hybrid materials were successfully prepared by a condensation reaction (Fig. 5a). Significantly, this work proves for the first time that COFs and MOFs were connected by chemical covalent amide bonds, which further shows multiple strategies for combining between MOFs and COFs, providing new insights for the further design of MOF/COF composites.Until now, however, most of the synthesized MOF/COF composites were constructed from MOFs with \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups in the ligands by covalent bonding with Schiff base reaction between the COF components, which greatly hinders further combination of MOFs and COFs to design MOF/COF composites with new structures and functions. Therefore, the development of a method to prepare MOF/COF hybrids utilizing MOFs without \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups is of great significance to further expand the integration between MOFs and COFs. Recently, Zhang et al. [69] developed a new method for the successful preparation of MOF@COF core-shell materials by postsynthetic modification of without \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) containing MOFs in the structures. In this work, MOF-808 was selected as a typical MOFs without \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) ligands, which was modified via p-aminobenzoic acid to substitute original acetate on the \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) cluster of MOF-808, thus \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups were successfully grafted onto its surface. Next, MOF@COF hybrids were synthesized as described in the previous section, using covalent linkages between the postmodified \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups on MOFs and COFs components via in-situ condensation. (Fig. 5b). Finally, to verify the generality of this synthesis method, covalently linked UiO-66@TpPa-1-COF was successfully assembled again using UiO-66 without \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups as the starting material.Furthermore, to enhance stability of COFs shells in core-shell MOF/COF composites under severe conditions, Peng et al. [70] synthesized a new functional porous material, aza-MOF@COF, by an aza-Diels-Alder cycloaddition reaction through post-synthetic modification (Fig. 5c). In this work, the MOFs were first functionalized (UiO-66 (CHO)) using a condensation reaction of the \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups in MOFs with the components used to synthesize Schiffbase COFs. In the second step, MOF@COF-LZU1 hybrid materials were successfully synthesized through condensation reaction of surface-functionalized MOFs with p-phenylenediamine. In the last step, the post-synthetic modification of MOF@COF-LZU1 was completed by the aza-Diels-Alder cycloaddition reaction between aryl imine and phenylacetylene, so that the imine bridges in MOF@COF-LZU1 composites were successfully transformed into the corresponding quinoline-linked aza-MOF@COF, thus the stability of the MOF@COF composites was greatly improved. In summary, the assembly strategy of post-synthesized modified MOFs, COFs or MOF/COF composites may provide new insights for the preparation of more covalently bonded MOF/COF hybrid materials.
