interaction between them provides a new way to fabricate MOF/ COF composites. For instance, Gao et al. [71] used this strategy to construct a hierarchical and structurally stable core-shell MOF@COF composite. In this work, PCN-222-Co was chosen as the core, p-phenylenediamine (Pa) was evenly dispersed on its surface by sonication, and then 1, 3, 5-triformylphloroglucinol (Tp) was added to react with Pa to prepare COFs shell; thus, the MOF@COF
composite was successfully synthesized by \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions between them (Fig. 6a). This strategy greatly expands the types of existing MOF/COF composites, but there are still some shortcomings, such as incomplete coverage or uneven deposition of COFs materials on the surface of MOFs crystals. Therefore, to overcome these problems, Yao et al. [72] modified the surface of MOFs seeds by adding polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) and branched
polyethyleneimine (BPEI), and realized the controllable synthesis of core-shell structure MOF/COF composites. Among them, the addition of PVP played a role in the uniform dispersion of MOFs seeds in the COFs reaction fluid, and BPEI provided crucial amino groups for the formation of imine COFs shells. This strategy is not only applicable to the controllable preparation of MOF/COF composites, but similar approach can also be employed for other composites
composed of COFs materials.
In conclusion, the above studies systematically introduce the current main synthetic strategies for the combination of MOFs and COFs. However, because of the diverse structures and species of MOFs and COFs, the synthesis of MOF/COF composites is still constantly being explored. For example, COFs can be used as
templates to restrict the growth of MOFs internally to build a new assembly strategy. Recently, Fan et al. [73] proposed a MOF-in-COF assembly strategy that used impregnation process to restrict the growth of MOFs in a supported COF layer template to synthesize advanced MOF-in-COF molecular sieve membranes for selective gas separation (Fig. 6b). Moreover, Feng et al. [74] applied the
strategy of modular total synthesis to framework materials for the first time and obtained a series of COF@MOF composites with coreshell structures (Fig. 6c). In this work, under the guidance of the assembly sequence determined by coordination or covalent bond strengths, MOFs and COFs were successfully connected hierarchically in reticular chemistry. With regard to the above discussion, it can be found that there are many synthetic strategies for MOF/ COF composites, but many are still in their infancy and need further exploration and efforts.
## 3. Analytical applications of MOF/COF composites
Owing to the efficient combination of diverse species of MOFs and COFs, the as-synthesized MOF/COF composites not only inherit their respective advantages but also exhibit some new functionalities because of the synergistic effects between them. Next, the latest developments in some typical and emerging applications of MOF/COF composites will be briefly highlighted, including analytical chemistry (membrane separation, sensing and sample pretreatment) and other fields (catalysis and energy storage) (Fig. 7).
## 3.1. Membrane separation
Compared with the main technologies used in commercial gas separations, such as low temperature distillation, pressure swing adsorption, temperature swing adsorption and chemical absorption, membrane-based gas separation is an attractive choice for energy-saving operation [75]. However, for traditional polymer membranes, there is still a trade-off between selectivity and permeability, which is known as Robeson's upper bound [76,77]. Therefore, the study of a new generation of new membrane materials that can exceed the upper bounds is an important research direction in the field of gas separation membranes. With the development of new technology and discovery of new materials, MOFs and COFs are considered to be very suitable for gas separation membrane processes because of their high porosity, uniform and tunable pore size, ultrahigh specific surface area and reasonable
thermal stability [47,78,79].
To explore the synergistic effects between different porous materials, Fu et al. [46] proved for the first time that MOFs can be grown on COFs membranes to fabricate a new type of composite membranes for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation. The resultant COF-MOF composite membranes, compared with the respective COFs and MOFs membranes, not only have higher separation selectivity (\~13) for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas mixtures, but also exceed the Robeson upper bound for the separation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas mixture pores by polymer membranes (Fig. 8a). Then, a series of characterizations, such as transmission electron microscopy (TEM), in situ energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) and fast Fourier transform (FFT), were carried out to study the reason why the membranes showed such excellent separation performance. Through analysis, it is found that the interlayer of composite membrane was composed of two parts, amorphous (MOFs) and crystalline (COFs), in which the amorphous material filled the space between the crystalline materials (Fig. 8b). It is precisely because of this that the pore size of the composite membranes was effectively adjusted, so that the composite membranes could not only maintain the permeability similar to the original COFs and MOFs membranes, but also enhance the selectivity of the composite membranes and realize the synergistic effects between different porous materials. In the following study, the group went on to synthesize another COF-MOF composite membrane, which exhibited better \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation selectivity (17.2) through the synergistic effect of COFs and MOFs [80]. Subsequently, the research team continued to report a mixed-dimensional preparation strategy, that is, a three-dimensional (3D) MOF-mediated method was used to prepare two-dimensional (2D) COF-based composite membranes for the first time [81]. This unique membrane structure has extremely high permeability because the \(\pi\)-stacked columns of 2D COFs provide a direct channel for gas transport. Besides, the interlayer formed by the synergy between MOFs and COFs endows the developed co preliminary mposite membrane with high selectivity for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas mixtures (32.9). The above-mentioned MOF/COF composite membranes have achieved some initial achievements in gas separation, but it can be seen that their excellent performance mainly relies on the interfacial interactions between the MOFs and COFs layers, while sieving properties of the remaining ordered pore structures has not been fully exploited and remains to be further explored.
Furthermore, MOFs and COFs, as two types of emerging porous materials, despite their many similarities in structures and properties, are used in the field of membrane separation for the separation of different targets due to their pore size differences. For example, MOFs membranes are often used for gas separation due to their small pore sizes \((0.2{-}1\ \mathrm{nm})\), while the pore sizes \((1{-}5\ \mathrm{nm})\) of COFs membranes are much larger than the gas molecular diameter \((0.25{-}0.50\ \mathrm{nm})\), which seriously hinders its progress in the field of gas separation [82–86]. Therefore, to enhance the selective performance of COFs molecular sieving membranes, Fan et al. [73] proposed the concept of MOF-in-COF which confines the growth of MOFs (ZIF-67) with size close to or smaller than the COFs pore sizes (TpPa-1) within their pore size to fabricate MOF-in-COF membranes (ZIF-67-in-TpPa-1) with smaller pore sizes (Fig. 6b). The MOF-in-COF membrane was placed in a homemade module by the Wicke-Kallenbach method, and the selective separation performance of the composite membrane for single gases and equimolar binary mixtures were tested at \(298\ \mathrm{~K~}\) and 1 bar, respectively (Fig. 8c). As shown in Fig. 8d, the composite membrane not only has high \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) permeance \((>\!3000\) GPU), but also the real mixed gas separation factors for equimolar \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}\) gas pairs can reach 34.9, 33.3, 110.5, and 192.7, respectively, which is better than pure COFs membrane and MOFs membrane. In
conclusion, this MOF-in-COF assembly strategy provides an intriguing way for pore-space partitioning of COFs and may inspire the design of molecular sieving membranes with precise sieving properties. In addition, MOF/COF composites can also be used as fillers for mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) to improve the compatibility between polymers and fillers. For example, Cheng et al. [87] constructed MOF@COF (UiO-66-NH2@TpPa-1) hybrid fillers by coating chemically stable COFs layers on size-selective MOFs cores, and explored their application as new fillers in MMMs for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) separation (Fig. 9a). The results show that the high affinity between pure organic COFs layers and the polymer matrix effectively prevents the formation of voids at the nonselective interface and alleviates the agglomeration of MOFs fillers. More importantly, compared with the pure polymer membrane, due to the synergistic effect of size selective MOFs pores and polymer chain rigidification, only 5 wt% MOF@COF fillers were added, and the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity of MMMs increased by 48% and 79%, respectively (Fig. 9b and c).
In addition to the above-mentioned use of MOF/COF membranes for the separation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), Liu et al. [88] recently fabricated a MOF-COF "alloy" membrane by electrochemical codeposition method, achieving high-efficiency separation of propylene/propane. Specifically, by incorporating different amounts of COFs into the ZIF-8 matrix, the Coulomb force between the two is utilized to restrict the linker rotation of ZIF-8, thereby effectively controlling the lattice rigidity of ZIF-8 to improve the sieving capability. The strategy, which exploits the synergistic effects between MOFs and COFs, effectively improved the problem of poor molecular sieving ability of MOFs due to linker rotation. Combining the above examples, it can be found that composite membranes prepared by the combination of MOFs and COFs exhibit better separation performance in gas selective separation due to the synergistic effects.
controlling the lattice rigidity of ZIF-8 to improve the sieving capability. The strategy, which exploits the synergistic effects between MOFs and COFs, effectively improved the problem of poor molecular sieving ability of MOFs due to linker rotation. Combining the above examples, it can be found that composite membranes prepared by the combination of MOFs and COFs exhibit better separation performance in gas selective separation due to the synergistic effects.
## 3.2. Sensing
MOFs and COFs can immobilize aptamers by hydrogen bonds, electrostatic forces, stacking aromatic rings, van der Waals interactions, and covalent bonds, which provides a new platform for the preparation of electrochemical (EC) aptasensors for biosensing applications [49]. Compared with pure MOFs or COFs sensors, such MOF/COF hybrids based aptasensors can significantly amplify the EC signal response due to the intrinsic advantages of combining the composite materials and aptamers, resulting in a more sensitive and rapid response for ultratrace analyte detection. Recently, a series of MOF/COF composites have been synthesized to load aptamers to prepare EC aptasensors for ultrasensitive detection of various analytes. For example, Liu et al. [89] added the prepared TPN-COF into the preparation system of Co-MOF, and successfully prepared a novel MOF@COF hybrid for the construction of
ultrasensitive and selective detection of ampicillin (AMP) residues in various practical samples EC aptasensor (Fig. 10a). The resultant composites not only possess high stability and high specific surface area, but also firmly anchor aptamer chains on it through strong \(\pi\)-\(\pi\) stacking and hydrogen bonding, resulting in the fabrication of MOF@COF-based aptasensor with excellent selectivity and low detection limit. Then, the group also reported another novel CeMOF@COF hybrid nanomaterials-based label-free EC aptasensor, which have the characteristics of high selectivity, great reproducibility and stability [90]. Additionally, the aptasensor displayed excellent analytical performance and can detect ultra-low oxytetracycline residues in milk, wastewater and urine samples with high sensitivity.
In addition, Zhang et al. [91] successfully synthesized MOF@COF core-shell composites that can be utilized to develop ultra-sensitive EC aptasensors by covalently linking microporous MOFs (NH2–UiO66) and mesoporous COFs (Fig. 10b). Importantly, the aptasensor significantly improved the EC detection performance of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) and chloramphenicol (CAP), which is due to the high affinity between the phosphate groups of aptamers and dense \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV})\) sites of MOFs, and strong \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interaction between aptamers and COFs to bring about high immobilization of aptamer chains. Furthermore, Li et al. [92] reported another novel CuMOF@TpBD-COF core-shell nanocomposite as a label-free EC aptasensor for ultrasensitive and specific detection of platelet-derived growth factor-BB in human serum samples. The MOFs cores, because of its good electroactivity, serve as redox medium and directly generate a detectable electrochemical signals. Meanwhile, the porous COFs shell can not only protect the morphology of MOFs cores from being destroyed, but also enhance the immobilization of aptamer chains on its surface and their penetration into the pore channels, thus achieving sensitive detection of analytes. And other MOF/COF composites have also been successfully
conceived and prepared to promote their further application as aptasensors in the field of EC detection [93–95].
Apart from the above-mentioned EC aptasensors, MOF/COF composites have also made a preliminary development in the field of fluorescence-based sensors because of their excellent fluorescence response. For example, Wang et al. [96] proposed to eliminate the aggregation-caused quenching (ACQ) phenomenon due to low emission of COFs by forming MOF@COF composites (Fig. 11a). The hydrogen bonds formed between the prepared MOF@COF composites realize excited-state intramolecular proton transfer, so multiemission can be observed in the composites. As shown in Fig. 11b, when \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) is added into the composites, the emission of MOFs is enhanced, while the emission of COFs remains unchanged. However, when adenosine-5'-triphosphate (ATP) is added, the enol state of MOFs and COFs is improved, while the keto state of COFs remains stable. As a result, the ratiometric fluorescence sensing and visible detection of \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) and ATP have been realized successfully, and they can also be distinguished from each other. In summary, although the development of MOF@COF composites in the sensing field is still in their infancy, the above successful applications show promising application prospect for further fabrication of simple, sensitive and selective aptasensors.
## 3.3. Sample pretreatment
MOFs and COFs show excellent separation performance as adsorbents in sample pretreatment due to their huge specific surface area, tunable pore size, and ultra-high porosity [97,98]. Therefore, in recent years, researchers have also developed MOF/COF composites as adsorbents with excellent performance for extracting various compounds from complex matrices (Table 1). For instance, Chen et al. [99] synthesized \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-68@COF hybrid material for the determination of sulfonamides (SAs) residues in tap water, milk
and pork samples by pipette tip solid-phase extraction (PT-SPE) combined with high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC). The \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-68@COF composites exhibited excellent adsorption ability for SAs due to strong \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction, porous structure and hydrophobic interaction. Additionally, the MOF-5/COF (M5C) hybrid was proposed and developed as a robust adsorbent for simultaneous removal of auramine O (AO) and RhB cationic dyes from aqueous solution [100]. The fabricated M5C adsorbent displayed an adsorption capacity for AO and RhB at pH 9.5 are 17.95 and \(16.18\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. This was mainly ascribed to the fact that AO and RhB dyes were encapsulated into the cavities of M5C, which can greatly enhance the adsorption efficiency by binding with M5C through electrostatic, hydrogen bonding, Lewis acid-base, and \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interactions (Fig. 12a). In addition, Li et al.
