[101] prepared a \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF@COF}\) magnetic composite for selective separation and preconcentration of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) by a magnetic solid phase extraction (MSPE) method coupled with UV-vis spectrophotometry. The adsorption capacity of the magnetic composite can reach up to \(37.29\ \mathrm{mg\ g^{-1}}\), which was significantly higher than that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}\) (16.83 mg \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)), and it exhibited high adsorption selectivity for \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) in multicomponent solutions (\(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\), \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\), \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\)). Meanwhile, Zhong et al. [102] prepared core-shell \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti)@TpBa-1 composites by solvothermal method for the removal of radionuclides from solution. The adsorbent has high adsorption capacity for \(\mathrm{UO}_{2}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\), which can be as high as \(536.73\ \mathrm{mg/g}\) and \(593.97\ \mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively. Among them, the high efficiency adsorption performance of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125(Ti)@TpPa-1 adsorbent for radionuclides is attributed to the nitrogen/oxygen-containing functional groups in the composites, which endow it with chelation and electrostatic attraction (Fig. 12b). What's more, some other MOF/COF composites have also been successfully synthesized as solid-phase adsorbents for other pollutants or residual drugs in complex matrices [103,104], which will further broaden the application potential of MOFs and COFs in sample preparation.
Recently, a novel MOF@COF composites were constructed by combining hexahedral-shaped \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and spherical COF–V and used as dispersive solid-phase extraction (d-SPE) adsorbent
Recently, a novel MOF@COF composites were constructed by combining hexahedral-shaped \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and spherical COF–V and used as dispersive solid-phase extraction (d-SPE) adsorbent for highly efficient extraction of trace phenoxy carboxylic acids (PCAs) from water and food samples (Fig. 13a) [105]. Coupled with liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) technique, an efficient, accurate and sensitive method for PCA detection was established. The reason why these composites could rapidly enrich PCAs is the \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction, hydrogen bond, hydrophobic effects, and halogen bond between UiO-66@COF–V composite and PCAs (Fig. 13b). Furthermore, MOF@COF composites were recently immobilized on corncob (corncob@UiO-66-NH2@TpBD) to improve their adsorption capacity (Fig. 13c) [106]. The corncob@UiO-66-NH2@TpBD was applied as an adsorbent for in-syringe solid phase extraction (D-SPE) combined with ultraperformance liquid chromatography (UPLC) to extract SAs from food samples and water. Owing to the good stability, large specific surface area and multiple interactions between the targets, the adsorbent exhibited excellent adsorption capacity.
Besides MOF@COF composites, the comparable COF@MOF composites have also been constructed for application in sample pretreatment. Jiang et al. [107] reported the controlled growth of spherical zinc-based MOFs (ZIF-8) on magnetic-modified COFs (\(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{TAPB-COF}\)) to synthesize a novel \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{TAPB-COF}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}\) nanocomposite for MSPE of trace bisphenols in functional beverages. The obtained nanocomposite showed excellent selectivity and high adsorption efficiency for bisphenols, which made it a
promising sorbent for selective isolation and enrichment of bisphenols. The above research expanded the application of composites combined with MOFs and COFs and showed great application value in sample pretreatment.
## 4. Other applications of MOF/COF composites
## 4.1. Catalysis
The integration of MOFs and COFs is a good strategy to optimize the performance of catalysts, which can not only achieve synergistic effect, but also eliminate the obstacles associated with the use of single components. For example, on the one hand, the porous structure of such composites can provide more active sites and channels for reactant transport. On the other hand, because of their molecular architecture and chemical modularity, strong interfacial interactions can be created, which are very important for charges transfer and separation [60]. Therefore, MOF/COF composites since their emergence, they have been greatly developed and applied in the field of catalysis, especially in the study of photocatalytic materials.
As discussed above, Lan's group has synthesized the first covalently integrated MOF/COF composites \(\mathrm{(NH_{2}{-}U i O{-}66/T p P a{-}1{-}C O F)}\) as photocatalyst for hydrogen evolution reaction (Fig. 4a) [62]. The obtained MOF/COF hybrid materials show excellent photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution rate, about 20 times higher than the parent COF, which is due to the well-matching band gaps between MOFs and COFs, the outstanding optical features of COFs, and the efficient charge separation across covalent heterojunction interface in the composites. Subsequently, a large number of MOF@COF composites have been synthesized and successfully used to enhance photocatalytic hydrogen evolution, such as \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)/B-CTF-1 [68], \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - UIO-66@TFP-DETH [60], MOF-808@TpPa-1-COF [69], MTV-TiMOF/COF [108], and so on [109,110].
In addition to enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen evolution, MOF/COF composites also show predominant ability in photocatalytic degradation of pollutants and have made preliminary progress. Zhang's group synthesized a novel \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-68@TPA-COF core-shell hybrid material, which was used as an effective visible-light-driven photocatalyst for the degradation of Rhodamine B (RhB) (Fig. 4b) [63]. Compared with pure MOFs \((0.054\;\mathrm{min}^{-1})\) , the prepared MOF@COF hybrid \((0.077\,\mathrm{\min}^{-1})\) ) showed stronger photocatalytic degradation ability owing to its increased BET surface area and the smaller band gap. Recently, Zhong et al. [111] further explored its performance in the photocatalytic reduction of U(VI) using \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)@TpPa-1, which was previously used to adsorb and remove U(VI) [102]. The results showed that the as-prepared \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti)@TpPa-1 \((81.6\%)\) composites exhibited much higher photoreduction removal rates of U(VI) than \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL125(Ti) \((57.7\%)\) , which was attributed to the generated heterojunction at the MOF@COF interface. Similarly, MOF@COF photocatalysts for the degradation of methyl orange [112], Cr(VI) [113] and RhB [114] under visible light have also been successfully synthesized. The electronic and optical properties of the obtained MOF@COF photocatalysts were greatly facilitated, thereby significantly increasing their photodegradation rate.
With the exception of the above-mentioned photocatalysis field, MOF/COF composites can also be applied in enzyme catalysis. In recent years, the application of some MOF-based nanozymes in biomedicine has attracted interest [115,116]. For example, some MOFs with peroxidase features can act in a significant role in reactive oxygen species (ROS)-mediated bacterial elimination by catalyzing the conversion of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into highly toxic hydroxyl radicals (·OH), so they can serve as a promising antibacterial agent in nanomedicine and biomaterials [117]. Although promising, their catalytic and therapeutic efficiency are unsatisfactory due to some intrinsic defects compared with that of natural enzymes. Therefore, inspired by nature, Zhang et al. [118] employed a "sequential growth" strategy to synthesize a MOF@COF nanozyme as highly efficient peroxidase mimic to enhance the catalytic and therapeutic efficiency of MOF-based nanozymes. A series of in vitro and in vivo experimental studies have demonstrated that it has a strong bacterial inhibitory effect as well as can make injury rapid to heal up. Among other examples, diverse MOF@COF composites have also been constructed for application in heterogeneous catalysis and organic catalysis. For example, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Fe)@NTU-COF was used for styrene oxidation [64], \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125@TAPB-PDA for selective oxidation of alcohols [65], Pd/TiATA@LZU1, and TiMOFs@Pt@DM-LZU1 composites for tandem dehydrogenation and hydrogenation reactions [119,120], and so on [66,71,121]. Since MOF/COF composites are currently widely used in the field of catalysis, several reviews have discussed their application as catalysts in detail and will not be detailed here [53,54,122e125].
