## 4.2. Energy storage
Owing to the pioneering works of MOFs or COFs in the pyrolysis [126,127], they have become ideal precursors for carbonaceous materials with excellent electrochemical performance in energy storage such as lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) or capacitors. However, carbonaceous materials prepared from a single MOFs or COFs precursor have very limited functionalities [128,129]. Therefore, developing hybrid precursors that combine the advantages of these two carbon species to achieve synergistic effect is an effective strategy for improving the electrochemical performance of electrode materials. For example, Zhuang et al. [130] prepared a MOF@COF-derived porous carbon material \(\mathrm{(Co_{3}O_{4}/N P C)}\) by using one bottom-up synthetic strategy. The electrocatalytic measurements showed that highly dispersed \(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N P C}\) with desirable catalytic activity for the oxygen evolution reaction because of its activation and sieving properties for reactive substances. In addition, Sun et al. [131] designed a coordination-induced crosslinked COF/Mn-MOF composite with flower-like microsphere morphology for enhanced lithium-storage properties. Recently, Lin et al. [132] reported a novel sulfur cathode design strategy by coating TpPa-1-COF layer on \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66-derived carbon/transition metal composite. The obtained composite successfully solved the shuttle effect of lithium polysulfide and slow sulfur reaction kinetics, and effectively improved the electrochemical performance of lithium-sulfur batteries.
Beside the exploration of electrode materials for LIBs based on MOF@COF, Liu et al. [133] synthesized a representative core-shell MOF@COF hybrid material \(\mathrm{(NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}125@T P{-}D Q\ C O F)}\) as an ideal precursor for capacitive carbon materials, displayed high salt adsorption capacity and favorable cycling stability. Moreover, Peng et al. [70] also synthesized a new functional aza-MOF@COF hybrid porous material through post-synthetic modification of MOF@COF-LZU1, and employed it as electrodes in supercapacitors. The as-prepared electrode exhibited excellent supercapacitor performance is due to the enlargement of \(\pi\) -delocalization and the reduction of pore size in the hybrid structure, making it closer to the electrolyte ions. Subsequently, the research group demonstrated that utilizing the pores of MOF@COF composites can modulate the electrical transport properties, further revealing the potential of MOF@COF composites in energy storage applications [134]. Specifically, the redox-active 7,7,8,8-tetracyanoquinodimethane (TCNQ) molecules were infiltrated into the pores of MOF@COF to tune electrochemical performance, resulting in high-performance supercapacitor electrode materials. The results demonstrated that the as-synthesized MOF@COF-TCNQ
hybrid displayed outstanding performance as a supercapacitor electrode, i.e., high specific capacitance \((78.36\;\;\mathrm{mF\;\;cm}^{-2})\) and excellent stacking capacitance \((4.46\;\mathrm{F\;cm}^{-3})\), with a capacitance retention of \(86.4\%\) after 2000 cycles. The aforementioned studies showed that, MOF@COF composites have obvious advantages and synergistic effects over the individual MOFs or COFs components because of their favorable characteristics, which will enable them exhibit their brilliance in more fields.
## 5. Conclusions and perspectives
MOFs and COFs, as two emerging crystalline porous materials in framework materials, have been rapidly and vigorously developed for nearly two decades due to their structural features and dominant properties. However, they cannot individually meet the specific needs of various fields due to some of their intrinsic deficiencies. Therefore, to ameliorate the potential applications of most MOFs and COFs, many MOF- or COF-based composites have been developed. In this review, we systematically summarize the recent progress of MOF/COF composites with both MOFs and COFs characteristics. For example, dynamic covalent bonds for constructing COFs are introduced into MOFs to design and synthesize novel MOFs, or to prepare hybrid MOF/COF composites through covalent condensation or \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interaction between MOFs and COFs components. The composites obtained by the above strategies not only possess the structural features of the original components, but also often show unexpected new characteristics due to the synergistic effects between components, thereby having broad application potential in analytical chemistry and other fields. Nonetheless, the application of MOF/COF composites especially in analytical chemistry is still in its infancy, and further research is needed to fully exploit their potential.
Firstly, it is necessary to develop more kinds of MOFs materials to synthesize MOF/COF composites. For MOFs, water stability is critical to obtain satisfactory results in practical applications such as sample pretreatment. However, the current research mainly focuses on several common stable MOFs types, typically UiO-66, MIL125, and MIL-101. Therefore, COFs with good stability can be used as protective shells, and MOFs with poor water stability can be selected as cores to prepare core-shell materials to improve the stability of MOFs. Secondly, the different spatial sequence arrangements between MOFs and COFs should be further investigated and designed. At present, the reported MOF/COF composites are mainly prepared with MOFs as the core, namely MOF@COF composites. While for COFs, their applications in many target compounds are limited due to their limited selectivity and inherent hydrophobicity. Therefore, it is necessary to develop COF@MOF composites and MOF-in-COF materials with COFs as substrates to improve the defects of COFs. Thirdly, owing to the structure-function relationship of MOF/COF composites becomes more sophisticated, studies on the mechanisms of action between them and target analytes are very limited. Therefore, computational simulations and advanced characterization tools can be utilized to illustrate the roles of individual components and the specific mechanisms in complex applications. Thereby, different types of MOF/COF composites can be better guided and designed to meet the actual needs in different applications. Finally, the development and application of MOF/COF composites are still in their primary stage, and it is necessary to further exploit their potential in analytical applications. For example, MOF/COF composite membranes are currently mainly applied in gas separation, and their application in liquid phase separation remains to be explored. Additionally, MOF/COF composites with high specific surface area and predictable pore size are promising to be used as membranes or adsorbents for selective adsorption of pollutants (such as radionuclides, heavy metal ions, and pesticide residues). Furthermore, MOF/COF composites have not yet been used for chromatographic separation. Therefore, it is expected to combine MOFs and COFs with different hydrophilic/hydrophobic properties and pore sizes to separate target analytes with different polarities and sizes.
